WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.280
Marvel has been incredibly careful about making
sure that no real Avengers: Endgame spoilers

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.830
slip out prior to the film's April 26th release,
instituting strict controls over access to

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.710
the script, and scaring the stars into complete
radio silence.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:18.410
"I've been sort of put under surveillance
by Marvel."

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.759
"They don't want you talking about anything."

00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:24.480
"They got a lot of reach in the world."

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.240
So it's kind of ironic that one big spoiler
might have gotten out thanks to Marvel accidentally

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.179
including it in their own trailers!

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:32.800
Yes, it's true.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.440
Eagle-eyed fans pouring over every frame of
the recent Avengers: Endgame trailer discovered

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.329
that there's apparently a discrepancy between
the normal version and the 3-D version — and

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:46.570
that difference reveals the return of some
bad guys from Avengers: Infinity War.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:51.020
It happens during the scene where Jeremy Renner's
Hawkeye runs down a tunnel to escape a big

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.220
explosion.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.020
Nothing spoilery there, right?

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:59.281
But Reddit user andresgarcia102 discovered
that in the 3-D version of the trailer, two

00:00:59.281 --> 00:01:01.640
figures can be seen chasing him.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:05.470
Based on the appearance of the figures, it
appears as though the bad guys are none other

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:10.330
than the Outriders — the henchmen in Thanos's
employ who aided in the assault on Wakanda

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.189
during Infinity War.

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:15.950
What's interesting here isn't just the fact
that the Outriders seem to be rearing their

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:21.100
ugly, xenomorph-esque heads in Endgame — it's
also that they aren't seen in the 2D Endgame

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.850
trailer, only in the 3D footage.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.170
This suggests pretty strongly that Marvel
may have purposefully altered the footage

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.990
in the normal trailer in order to prevent
any spoilers from leaking.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:36.020
But apparently this detail was missed when
they were editing the 3D version — and naturally,

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.270
Marvel's rabid Avengers fans discovered it
right away.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:44.190
This is far from the first time Marvel filmmakers
— namely Joe and Anthony Russo, who are

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:48.729
responsible for Infinity War, Endgame, and
Captain America: Civil War — have screwed

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:53.070
around with footage in their trailers as a
means to keep plot details protected.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:57.500
Most famously, the first trailers for Infinity
War included a sequence where the Avengers

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:01.049
team, which had assembled to defend Wakanda,
included the Hulk.

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:06.229
However, Hulk isn't anywhere to be seen during
the climactic battle, because he refused to

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:11.090
participate, forcing Bruce Banner to fight
using Iron Man's Hulkbuster armor.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.970
Co-director Anthony Russo admitted that they
made up the scene with the Hulk just for the

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:15.970
trailer.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.920
"That shot [...] was never in the movie in
the version that you saw.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.690
It was never even created for the movie in
that version.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.120
It was literally created in that version for
the trailer."

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.060
And his brother, co-director Joe Russo, said
they do that stuff all the time.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.570
"We look at the trailer as a very different
experience than the movie, and I think audiences

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:38.220
are so predictive now that you have to be
very smart about how you craft a trailer because

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.640
an audience can watch a trailer and basically
tell you what’s gonna happen in the film.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.510
[...] So at our disposal are lots of different
shots that aren’t in the movie that we can

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:50.950
manipulate through CG to tell a story that
we want to tell specifically for the purpose

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.760
of the trailer and not for the film."

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.830
That does raise the possibility that the presence
of these Outriders are also an elaborate fake-out.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.740
But considering they aren't in the normal
version of the trailer, it seems more likely

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:07.000
that they were erased from that version rather
than added to the 3D version.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.520
Whatever the case, the big takeaway is that
even if this spoiler turns out to be correct,

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.380
you should take everything in the trailer
with a grain of salt, because any part of

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:16.980
it could be bogus.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.100
Some fans, for instance, are already speculating
that the shot of Tony Stark walking with the

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:25.460
team in the 2nd trailer is a fake-out — and
we know that scene was heavily edited one

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.280
way or another, because a different version
with different characters appeared in the

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.000
first Super Bowl teaser.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.160
There's only one way to know the truth, of
course, so… we'll see you in the theaters.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.360
Looks like the trailers did their job after
all.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.440
"Mark, that is enough!

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.380
That is enough spoilers!

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.720
We have to see the movie!"

